            <ArticleTitle>Matching patient-specific expectations with physician-specific outcomes: towards transparency in treatment decision-making and consent.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1287-91</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This article hypothesizes that while the principles of evidence-based practice should serve as a guiding ideal for physicians, the manner in which this practice style is commonly implemented may be insufficient to the provision of truly informed decision-making and consent - especially in cases where outcomes are inextricably tied to technique, such as in the surgical subspecialities. Specifically, current methods of 'shared decision making' fail to adequately incorporate patient-specific expectations and physician-specific outcomes. A 'next-step', aimed at matching such expectations with outcomes is suggested and a method for implementation is presented.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
